From e3a6891d0d5904bc8b07fc906509bfb0eaae2855 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 06:59:56 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0071 -- midi_sched_tick() call-site cost
 diagnostic (S7-GUS)

WHY: real hardware (tags GUST1/GUSD1) found the MIDI slot Poll() loop's
own slots_us at a mean of ~102ms and a max of ~6.89 SECONDS, ONE single
call, nearly identical across two independent runs (175us apart out of
6.89s -- a deterministic mechanism, not I/O jitter). SDL patch SDL/0146
+ ags patch 0070 already bracket every individual SDL_DOSGus* voice call
gus_sink.c's note-on/note-off/reap/on_tick paths make; this brackets
the ONE call slots_us actually times end-to-end -- midi_sched_tick(),
called once per real tick from AudioPlayer::MidiPoll() (itself called
from audio_core_entry_poll()'s slot loop, the exact loop slots_us
wraps) -- so the real-hardware run can confirm whether the five
per-voice-call snapshots fully account for the outlier or whether a
residual points at something else inside midi_sched_tick() itself (the
delta_ms>10000 catch-up clamp, the dispatch for(;;) loop, or SDL_
DOSGusAllocVoice(), none of which are separately instrumented).

WHAT: audioplayer.cpp (this port's own file) brackets the midi_sched_
tick() CALL SITE inside AudioPlayer::MidiPoll() with a uclock()-based
accumulate-and-snapshot pair, DOSAGS_MidiTickDiagSnapshot() -- same
idiom as every other diagnostic in this investigation. Deliberately
does NOT instrument midi_sched_tick() itself (shared/audio/midi_sched.c,
hub-owned code every port consumes via the .sdl-dos-ports/ subtree or
submodule): a temporary, dosags-specific diagnostic has no business
rippling into shared/ when bracketing the call site achieves the exact
same measurement without touching it at all. audio_core.cpp folds the
new accessor into the existing DOS-PORT audioprb log line as a sixth
new field, miditick(...), alongside voicefreq/voicevol/voicepan/
voicestart/voicestop (patch 0070) and gusscan (patch 0069).

Temporary -- to be reverted once this sub-investigation concludes.

